WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.610
Part one.

00:00:01.610 --> 00:00:03.768
Why rural data is different.

00:00:04.318 --> 00:00:07.638
Here's a real puzzle from rural Alabama.

00:00:07.638 --> 00:00:10.208
A study published in 2017 reported

00:00:10.208 --> 00:00:16.978
that 19 of 55 stool samples from Lowndes County tested positive for hookworm DNA.

00:00:16.978 --> 00:00:19.968
Hookworm is a parasite of poor sanitation

00:00:19.968 --> 00:00:23.738
that many people assume the United States left behind.

00:00:23.738 --> 00:00:26.508
A larger study, published in 2023,

00:00:26.508 --> 00:00:32.208
tested stool from 704 children in the same region and found none.

00:00:32.208 --> 00:00:34.724
Two careful studies, two different answers.

00:00:35.276 --> 00:00:37.146
Why the difference?

00:00:37.146 --> 00:00:40.716
The first study went to homes chosen for poor sanitation

00:00:40.716 --> 00:00:43.436
and used a very sensitive test.

00:00:43.436 --> 00:00:46.856
The second tested children across the region.

00:00:46.856 --> 00:00:50.306
But both found the same problem underneath.

00:00:50.306 --> 00:00:55.846
About four in ten people in the first study reported raw sewage exposure at home.

00:00:55.846 --> 00:00:59.186
In the second, 12 percent of children lived in homes

00:00:59.186 --> 00:01:01.840
that discharge untreated wastewater.

00:01:02.401 --> 00:01:08.691
The area's clay soil drains poorly, so septic systems often fail, and a system

00:01:08.691 --> 00:01:13.601
that works can cost more than many families can afford.

00:01:13.601 --> 00:01:16.651
A clinic's records could never show you that.

00:01:16.651 --> 00:01:21.361
You'd need health, environmental, and community data, kept by organizations

00:01:21.361 --> 00:01:24.221
that rarely work together.

00:01:24.221 --> 00:01:27.071
And you'd need local people.

00:01:27.071 --> 00:01:30.231
A community group helped run the first study.

00:01:30.231 --> 00:01:32.926
People who live there know things records don't.

00:01:33.485 --> 00:01:36.455
Two words describe this work.

00:01:36.455 --> 00:01:41.365
Curation means keeping each piece of data labeled and honest,

00:01:41.365 --> 00:01:46.505
like a museum curator writing the card beside an object: what it is,

00:01:46.505 --> 00:01:49.365
where it came from, and when.

00:01:49.365 --> 00:01:53.525
Integration means lining up data from different sources,

00:01:53.525 --> 00:01:55.832
so they describe the same place and time.

00:01:56.401 --> 00:01:59.361
All health data needs that care.

00:01:59.361 --> 00:02:02.351
So what makes rural data different?

00:02:02.351 --> 00:02:03.146
Three things.

00:02:03.695 --> 00:02:05.865
First, small numbers.

00:02:05.865 --> 00:02:08.965
Greene County has about 7,000 people.

00:02:08.965 --> 00:02:13.745
In a place that size, a handful of events can change a rate a lot.

00:02:13.745 --> 00:02:17.785
Official systems also hide small counts to protect privacy.

00:02:17.785 --> 00:02:22.275
A death-records database from the US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention,

00:02:22.275 --> 00:02:27.878
the CDC, hides local counts under ten, and you'll see the word suppressed instead.

00:02:28.445 --> 00:02:31.465
Second, thin coverage.

00:02:31.465 --> 00:02:36.485
We counted the US Environmental Protection Agency's list of monitors for fine

00:02:36.485 --> 00:02:42.215
particle pollution, the tiny specks in the air that can get deep into the lungs.

00:02:42.215 --> 00:02:46.455
More than one in three metro counties has an active monitor.

00:02:46.455 --> 00:02:50.445
Outside metro areas, it's about one in eleven.

00:02:50.445 --> 00:02:55.597
Greene County isn't on the list, so any air number for it has to be an estimate.

00:02:56.154 --> 00:02:59.914
Third, people cross county lines.

00:02:59.914 --> 00:03:04.464
In one report on eight states, using 2018 data,

00:03:04.464 --> 00:03:09.114
30 percent of hospital stays for rural patients were away from their local

00:03:09.114 --> 00:03:13.164
hospital, for care it could have provided.

00:03:13.164 --> 00:03:18.094
So a county's clinic records don't describe that county's people,

00:03:18.094 --> 00:03:20.604
and a needed clinic can look unneeded.

00:03:21.154 --> 00:03:23.394
Why does this matter now?

00:03:23.394 --> 00:03:28.624
Since 2005, at least 197 rural hospitals have closed

00:03:28.624 --> 00:03:31.514
or stopped offering hospital stays.

00:03:31.514 --> 00:03:36.464
For working-age adults, the death rate from disease outside metro areas was 6

00:03:36.464 --> 00:03:42.834
percent above metro areas in 1999, and 43 percent above them in 2019.

00:03:42.834 --> 00:03:48.104
And a new federal program is sending 50 billion dollars to states over five years.

00:03:48.104 --> 00:03:50.341
Those decisions need careful evidence.

00:03:50.904 --> 00:03:52.874
Here's the good part.

00:03:52.874 --> 00:03:57.444
People in these places deserve honest numbers, and you can help.

00:03:57.444 --> 00:04:01.994
If you learn to read small numbers honestly here, you can read them anywhere:

00:04:01.994 --> 00:04:06.804
a small city neighborhood, a rare disease, a single clinic.

00:04:06.804 --> 00:04:09.323
Next, how to question a dataset.

